The Identifiability of Computational Descent

A Causal-Statistical Theory of Model Lineage Under Transformation and Partial Observation

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

This thesis reformulates the problem of detecting unauthorized descendants of large language models as a problem of causal identifiability and certification relative to a transformation class \mathcal{T}. We reject the prevailing engineering framing — in which the central object is a "detector" or "fingerprint" — and replace it with a structural framing in which the central objects are the quadruple

\mathfrak{P} = (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O})

of model class, transformation class, adversary class, and observation channel. The detector is shown to be downstream of the identifiability relation, not constitutive of it.

The thesis makes nine principal contributions.

First, we separate four notions of descent — computational, causal-computational, interventional, and historical — and prove that the naive chain

\text{Historical} \Rightarrow \text{Computational} \Rightarrow \text{Interventional}

is false at its second arrow. The correct chain requires an explicit dependence condition on the transformation map. We introduce the dependence functional \operatorname{Dep}_T(M) and prove that causal-computational descent is equivalent to non-null interventional descent under a specified metric on laws.

Second, we prove an Opacity Theorem in conditional form: certification is impossible exactly when the adversary class contains two processes that are observationally equivalent under the channel but carry opposite descent labels. This reformulation is robust to the empirically documented possibility that substitution may be detectable in some regimes (IRIS) while remaining non-certifiable in general.

Third, we develop the correct Le Cam hierarchy for the lineage problem, separating sum, Bayes, and minimax risks with their exact two-point bounds, and we identify the composite-hypothesis structure of the lineage decision problem.

Fourth, we correct the Fano-theoretic analysis. The classical inequality is stated only for identification, not certification; the data-processing floor is derived in its correct direction; and the adaptive Fano bound is stated in conditional form, with the pool size K \ge 2 almost surely.

Fifth, we prove monotonicity of the total-variation functional \Delta_n under nested \sigma-algebras, exhibit its failure under non-nesting summaries, and give a rigorous convergence theorem expressing \Delta_n \uparrow \|P_A^{(\infty)} - P_B^{(\infty)}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Sixth, we replace the scalar invariant map \mathcal{I} : \mathcal{M} \to \Sigma with a relational provenance predicate

\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_1, \dots, M_k, M'; \pi)

that correctly handles single-parent and multi-parent operations under a unified formalism.

Seventh, we separate certification from private certification: the zero-knowledge property is a privacy-preserving refinement, not a logical prerequisite. The Authenticated Provenance Invariant is accordingly split into a five-property core and a six-property extended form.

Eighth, we unify single-parent and multi-parent transformations under

\mathcal{T} : \bigsqcup_{k \ge 1} \mathcal{M}^k \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M}

and identify the construction or refutation of an API for a specified \mathcal{T} as the central open problem.

Ninth, we introduce the identifiability poset \mathfrak{I}(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) — deliberately not called a lattice until meet and join are shown to exist — and prove that it is non-increasing along the observation hierarchy for fixed adversary and loss.

The thesis concludes that the primary object of study is not the detector but the pair (\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}), and that the next mathematical frontier is the formalization of \mathcal{T} without circularity, under the constraint that single-parent and multi-parent operations share one formalism.

