Introduction

The problem

A large language model M is trained. Through fine-tuning, quantization, distillation, pruning, merging, or recursive replication, a new model M' appears. The question is whether M' is a descendant of M, and if so, whether this fact can be certified in a form that survives adversarial scrutiny.

The empirical literature has attacked this problem from several directions. Behavioral fingerprinting extracts statistical signatures of model identity from black-box outputs. Weight-embedding provenance inserts persistent markers into parameters. Hardware attestation binds an execution to a trusted environment. Cryptographic inference proofs bind an output to a committed model. Each of these addresses a fragment of the problem. None of them poses the problem as a question of identifiability under an admissible transformation class.

The consequences of this fragmentation are not merely taxonomic. Without a formal object \mathcal{T} of admissible transformations, statements of the form "model M' is a descendant of model M" have no mathematical content. Without a formal adversary class \mathcal{A}, the notion of a "successful detection" is not well-posed. Without an explicit observation channel \mathcal{O}, the claim that a method "works" is not falsifiable. The empirical literature has, in effect, been solving a family of optimization problems without having posed the decision problem they are supposed to solve.

This thesis poses the decision problem.

The central reframing

We replace the question

"How do we detect that an LLM has escaped?"

with the question

"For which quadruples (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}) is descent identifiable, and for which is it certifiable?"

This shift has several immediate consequences.

First, negative results become as valuable as positive ones. A proof that no certification procedure can distinguish two hypotheses under a specified channel is not a failure of engineering; it is a theorem about the limits of the problem.

Second, the transformation class \mathcal{T} becomes the central object. Everything else — the model class, the adversary class, the observation channel — is defined relative to \mathcal{T}.

Third, the detector becomes downstream of the identifiability relation. The detector is a decision rule; the identifiability relation is the structure that determines whether such a rule can exist.

The four notions of descent

A central difficulty in the existing literature is that "descendant" is used to mean at least four different things, and the differences matter.

A model M' may be a computational descendant of M if it was produced by a program that took M as an input. This is the weakest notion: the program need not have used M.

A model M' may be a causal-computational descendant of M if the computation actually depended on M in a measurable sense.

A model M' may be an interventional descendant of M if there exists an intervention on M that changes the law of M'. This is the standard causal notion, and it is not equivalent to the computational one.

A model M' may be a historical descendant of M if there exists an actual realized trajectory from M to M' through a sequence of admissible transformations.

Version 0.3 of this work asserted the chain

\text{Historical} \Rightarrow \text{Computational} \Rightarrow \text{Interventional}.

The second implication is false. A transformation T may receive M as input but produce T(M; R) = N independent of M. We exhibit this counterexample in Chapter 2 and repair the hierarchy.

The central obstruction

The Opacity Theorem, in its conditional form, states:

Certification fails exactly when the adversary class contains two processes that are observationally equivalent under \mathcal{O} but carry opposite descent labels.

This is almost tautological. Its value is not in its content but in its localization: it identifies the failure mode precisely, and it separates the failure into two mechanisms — confounding and substitution — that the empirical literature has treated as one.

The confounding mechanism is classical: a common cause C of both M and M' induces dependence without descent. The substitution mechanism is adversarial: a provider serves a model M'' \neq M' designed to be distributionally indistinguishable from M' under \mathcal{O}.

Both are captured by the same observational-equivalence argument. The substitution mechanism is not a confounding problem in the Pearl sense; it is an adversarial construction. Recent empirical work (IRIS) shows that substitution is sometimes detectable from output text alone. This does not weaken the theorem: the theorem says that substitution is not universally certifiable, and this is consistent with its being sometimes detectable.

The certification problem

Given an admissible transformation class \mathcal{T} and an observation channel \mathcal{O}, we ask whether there exists a certification procedure C : \mathcal{Y}^n \times \Pi \to \{\text{valid}, \text{invalid}\} such that

\Pr[C(Y^n, \pi) = \text{valid} \mid M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)] \ge 1 - \epsilon,

\Pr[C(Y^n, \pi) = \text{valid} \mid M' \notin \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)] \le \epsilon + \mathsf{negl}(\lambda).

The certification procedure consumes both an observation Y^n \sim \mathcal{O}(M') and a proof \pi. The proof serves to authenticate claims that cannot be verified from the observation alone.

The thesis argues that the correct object of study is not a scalar invariant \mathcal{I}(M) \in \Sigma — which cannot handle multi-parent operations such as merging — but a relational provenance predicate

\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_1, \dots, M_k, M'; \pi).

For k = 1, this reduces to a monogamous descent relation. For k > 1, it captures merging and ensemble-derived transformations. For chains, it composes along trajectories.

The transformation class problem

The transformation class \mathcal{T} is the central unresolved object of the thesis. We impose four requirements:

· (R1) Non-circular. Membership in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) must be definable without reference to "descendant."
· (R2) Non-trivial. \mathcal{T} \neq \{\mathrm{id}\} and \mathcal{T} must not collapse to the whole model space.
· (R3) Closed under composition. Trajectories are representable.
· (R4) Realistic. \mathcal{T} contains fine-tuning, quantization, distillation, pruning, merging, and recursive replication.

We propose the unification

\mathcal{T} : \bigsqcup_{k \ge 1} \mathcal{M}^k \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M},

where k is the number of parents. Single-parent transformations are k = 1; merging is k \ge 2; composition is defined by concatenation of trajectories.

Whether there exists a resource-bounded version of \mathcal{T} that satisfies (R1)–(R4) and admits an API is the central open problem.

Contributions in summary

The thesis is organized around nine contributions, distributed across ten chapters.

Chapter 1 establishes notation and primitives: models as measurable maps, resources as auxiliary bundles, and transformations as partial functions on \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R}.

Chapter 2 introduces the four notions of descent, proves the corrected hierarchy, and exhibits the counterexample to the naive implication.

Chapter 3 develops the quotient-space theory of permutation-invariance and its statistical justification via the quotient feature space.

Chapter 4 defines observation channels, adversary classes, and the observation hierarchy \mathcal{O}_A \subset \mathcal{O}_B \subset \mathcal{O}_C, with \mathcal{O}_D treated as an attestation capability rather than a channel.

Chapter 5 proves the conditional Opacity Theorem and derives the impossibility of uniform certification for observation channels below the attestation level.

Chapter 6 develops the Le Cam hierarchy — sum, Bayes, minimax — with correct two-point bounds and the composite-hypothesis extension.

Chapter 7 states the classical and adaptive Fano inequalities in their correct directions and separates identification from certification.

Chapter 8 proves monotonicity of \Delta_n under nested \sigma-algebras, exhibits its failure under non-nesting summaries, and gives the convergence theorem.

Chapter 9 formulates the Authenticated Provenance Invariant in its five-property core (certification) and six-property extended form (private certification), proves the sufficiency theorem, and argues that zero-knowledge is a privacy refinement rather than a logical prerequisite.

Chapter 10 introduces the transformation class problem, unifies single-parent and multi-parent operations, defines the identifiability poset, and states the central open problems.

A note on method

This thesis is theoretical. It does not propose new algorithms. It does not benchmark methods. It does not claim empirical superiority over existing fingerprinting approaches.

What it does claim is the following: that the lineage problem has a natural mathematical formulation; that this formulation yields non-trivial impossibility results; that the impossibility results are sharp in a specified sense; and that the remaining open problems are well-posed and mathematically tractable.

The detector is not the problem. The primary object is (\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}).

