Chapter 1. Foundations

1.1 Models as measurable maps

Definition 1.1 (Model). A model is a triple

M = (\mathcal{X}_M, \mathcal{Y}_M, f_M),

where \mathcal{X}_M is an input space, \mathcal{Y}_M is an output space, and

f_M : \mathcal{X}_M \to \Delta(\mathcal{Y}_M)

is a measurable map into the space of probability distributions on \mathcal{Y}_M. We write Y \sim M(x) for Y \sim f_M(x).

Definition 1.2 (Internal state). To every model M we associate an internal state X_M \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{int}}, a measurable space carrying the parameters, architecture, tokenizer, and auxiliary state of M. The map M \mapsto X_M is assumed measurable.

Definition 1.3 (Model class). A model class \mathcal{M} is a set of models equipped with a \sigma-algebra \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{M}} such that the transformations below are measurable.

1.2 Resource spaces

Definition 1.4 (Resource space). A resource space is a measurable space (\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{R}}) whose elements are bundles R = (D, C, \rho, \mathbf{M}) of data D, compute C, randomness \rho, and other models \mathbf{M} = (M_1, \dots, M_{k-1}) \in \mathcal{M}^{k-1}.

The inclusion of other models in \mathcal{R} is essential. It is what makes multi-parent operations representable inside a single transformation formalism.

1.3 The unified transformation class

Definition 1.5 (Transformation class). A transformation class is a set

\mathcal{T} \subseteq \bigsqcup_{k \ge 1} \left( \mathcal{M}^k \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M} \right)

of measurable partial maps

T : \mathcal{M}^k \times \mathcal{R} \rightharpoonup \mathcal{M},

indexed by arity k, satisfying:

· (Arity compatibility). For each k \ge 1, the subset \mathcal{T}_k \subseteq \mathcal{T} of arity-k transformations is a measurable subset.
· (Composition). If T_1 \in \mathcal{T}_{k_1} and T_2 \in \mathcal{T}_{k_2} with M' \in \operatorname{dom}(T_1) and M' \in \operatorname{dom}(T_2), then the composite is representable in \mathcal{T}.
· (Resource closure). If T \in \mathcal{T} is admissible at R, and R' \le R in the natural resource order (fewer data, less compute), then T is admissible at R'.

Remark 1.6. Arity k = 1 corresponds to fine-tuning, quantization, distillation, pruning. Arity k \ge 2 corresponds to merging, ensembling, and stacked transfer. This unification is new relative to the literature, which treats merging as a separate operation.

1.4 Partial order on transformation classes

Definition 1.7. For two transformation classes \mathcal{T}_1, \mathcal{T}_2, we write \mathcal{T}_1 \preceq \mathcal{T}_2 if every transformation in \mathcal{T}_1 is admissible under \mathcal{T}_2 in the sense that

\forall T_1 \in \mathcal{T}_1, \exists T_2 \in \mathcal{T}_2, \forall (M_1, \dots, M_k, R) \in \operatorname{dom}(T_1), \quad T_1(M_1, \dots, M_k; R) = T_2(M_1, \dots, M_k; R').

This is a partial order on classes. It is generally not total: quantization and distillation are incomparable; merging is incomparable with pruning.

1.5 Permutation symmetry

Definition 1.8 (Parameter space). The parameter space V of a model class \mathcal{M} is the disjoint union of internal states X_M over M \in \mathcal{M}.

Definition 1.9 (Permutation group). Let G be a group of measure-preserving automorphisms of V, arising from layer-wise neuron permutations of the architectures in \mathcal{M}.

Definition 1.10 (Functional equivalence). Two models M_1, M_2 \in \mathcal{M} are permutation-equivalent, written M_1 \sim_G M_2, if there exists g \in G such that g \cdot X_{M_1} = X_{M_2}.

Lemma 1.11. \sim_G is an equivalence relation on \mathcal{M}.

Proof. Reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity follow from the group axioms. \square

Definition 1.12 (Quotient model space). The quotient model space is

\mathcal{M} / G := \{ [M] : M \in \mathcal{M} \}.

This is the correct space in which invariance claims should be formulated.

