Chapter 2. The Four Notions of Descent

2.1 Computational descent

Definition 2.1 (Computational descent). M' is a computational descendant of M under \mathcal{T}, written M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M), if there exist T \in \mathcal{T} and R \in \mathcal{R} such that

M' = T(M; R).

This asserts only that M was an argument of the computation. It does not assert that the output depended on the argument.

Counterexample 2.2. Let T(M; R) = N, where N \in \mathcal{M} is fixed and independent of M. Then M' = N \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) for every M. But the law of M' is constant in M, so the interventional effect of M on M' is null.

This counterexample is not artificial. A transformation may include a copy of M in its inputs for bookkeeping reasons and never use it, or may use M only in a branch that is dead under all admissible R.

2.2 Causal-computational descent

Definition 2.3 (Dependence functional). For T \in \mathcal{T} and R \in \mathcal{R}, define

\operatorname{Dep}_T(M; R) := \sup_{m_0, m_1 \in \mathcal{M}} d\!\left( \mathcal{L}(T(m_0; R)), \mathcal{L}(T(m_1; R)) \right),

where d is a metric on \Delta(\mathcal{M}) induced by an ambient metric on \mathcal{M}.

Definition 2.4 (Causal-computational descent). M' is a causal-computational descendant of M under \mathcal{T}, written M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{caus}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M), if M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) and there exists a realizing T \in \mathcal{T} and R \in \mathcal{R} with

\operatorname{Dep}_T(M; R) > 0.

Remark 2.5. The dependence functional is a robustness measure of the transformation in its first argument. It is zero if and only if T(\cdot; R) is constant in the first argument.

2.3 Interventional descent

Definition 2.6 (Interventional descent). M' is an interventional descendant of M under \mathcal{T}, written M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M), if

\exists m_0 \neq m_1 \in \mathcal{M}, \quad \mathcal{L}(M' \mid do(M = m_0)) \neq \mathcal{L}(M' \mid do(M = m_1)).

Proposition 2.7. \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{caus}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \subseteq \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) under the identification of \mathcal{M} with its distributional closure.

Proof sketch. If \operatorname{Dep}_T(M; R) > 0, then there exist m_0, m_1 with \mathcal{L}(T(m_0; R)) \neq \mathcal{L}(T(m_1; R)). Under the do-operator, M \mapsto T(M; R) defines the intervention, so the inequality lifts to the interventional law. \square

Proposition 2.8. \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \not\subseteq \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) in general.

Proof. Let C be a common cause of M and M'. Then M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) if interventions on M propagate through C, but M is not an argument of any transformation producing M'. \square

2.4 Historical descent

Definition 2.9 (Historical descent). M' is a historical descendant of M under \mathcal{T}, written M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{hist}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M), if there exists a finite trajectory

M = M_0 \xrightarrow{T_1, R_1} M_1 \xrightarrow{T_2, R_2} \cdots \xrightarrow{T_k, R_k} M_k = M'

with each T_i \in \mathcal{T} and each R_i \in \mathcal{R}.

Remark 2.10 (Non-circularity). Definition 2.9 does not use the word "descendant." It refers to transformations and resources, both primitives. The transitive closure implicit in the trajectory is definitional.

2.5 The corrected hierarchy

Theorem 2.11 (Corrected hierarchy). The following implications hold:

\boxed{\operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{hist}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \implies \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)}

\boxed{\operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{caus}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \implies \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)}

Neither \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}} \Rightarrow \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{caus}} nor \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}} \Rightarrow \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}} holds in general.

Proof. The first implication follows from taking k = 1 in the trajectory and observing that a single-step trajectory is a computational realization. The second is Proposition 2.7. The counterexamples are Proposition 2.8 and Counterexample 2.2. \square

Remark 2.12 (The naive chain is false). Version 0.3 asserted the chain

\text{Historical} \implies \text{Computational} \implies \text{Interventional}.

The second arrow fails. The corrected picture is a diamond: historical and causal-computational are incomparable refinements of computational, both implying the interventional apex under the appropriate closure assumptions.

2.6 The target of certification

We adopt historical descent as the target of certification. This choice is forced by the following argument.

Certification must be auditable: a verifier must be able to check that a specific trajectory occurred. Interventional descent is a counterfactual property and cannot be audited. Computational descent is too weak — it is satisfied by transformations that ignore their input. Causal-computational descent is closer to the target but loses the historical specificity: two different computational paths can induce the same dependence.

Historical descent is the unique notion that captures what actually happened.

