Chapter 3. Quotient-Space Representation

3.1 The invariance problem

A neural network's function is invariant under a large group of parameter permutations. Any invariant of model identity must respect this symmetry. The correct mathematical home for such invariants is the quotient space \mathcal{M}/G.

Theorem 3.1 (Factorization). Let \mathcal{I} : \mathcal{M} \to \Sigma be G-invariant:

\forall M_1, M_2 \in \mathcal{M}, \quad M_1 \sim_G M_2 \Rightarrow \mathcal{I}(M_1) = \mathcal{I}(M_2).

Then there exists a unique \bar{\mathcal{I}} : \mathcal{M}/G \to \Sigma with \bar{\mathcal{I}} \circ q = \mathcal{I}, where q : \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{M}/G is the quotient map.

Proof. This is the universal property of the quotient. \square

3.2 Statistical justification

The quotient is not merely a mathematical convenience. Sannai, Imaizumi, and Kawano (2021) proved that the volume of the quotient feature space controls the generalization error of invariant networks, and that the improvement over non-invariant bounds is by a factor of \sqrt{n!} for DeepSets. This establishes that quotienting has a provable statistical benefit.

3.3 The merging subtlety

Proposition 3.2 (Non-linearity of naive invariants). Let M' = \alpha M_1 + (1-\alpha)M_2 for \alpha \in (0, 1). Then for a general invariant \mathcal{I},

\mathcal{I}(M') \neq \alpha \mathcal{I}(M_1) + (1-\alpha) \mathcal{I}(M_2).

Proof. Choose \mathcal{I}(M) = \|X_M\|_{\ell^2}. Then \mathcal{I}(M') = \|\alpha X_{M_1} + (1-\alpha) X_{M_2}\|_{\ell^2}, which equals the right-hand side only if X_{M_1} and X_{M_2} are collinear. \square

Corollary 3.3. A scalar invariant \mathcal{I} : \mathcal{M} \to \Sigma satisfying \mathcal{I}(T(M)) = \mathcal{I}(M) for all T \in \mathcal{T} cannot consistently handle multi-parent operations. This motivates the relational formulation of Chapter 9.

Remark 3.4 (What Fréchet averaging does and does not justify). Recent work on quotient Fréchet averaging shows that, in the presence of symmetries, the appropriate merging object is a Fréchet mean on \mathcal{M}/G. This justifies quotient-aware merging, but it does not justify the claim that any provenance invariant must be linear under merging. The distinction matters: linearity is a property of specific invariants, not of the geometry of \mathcal{M}/G.

