Chapter 4. Observation Channels and Adversary Classes

4.1 Observation channels

Definition 4.1 (Observation channel). An observation channel is a measurable map

\mathcal{O} : \mathcal{M} \to \Delta(\mathcal{Y}), \quad M \mapsto Y.

We distinguish four levels:

\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{O}_A &: \text{output text only}, \\
\mathcal{O}_B &: \mathcal{O}_A + \text{timing (latency, inter-token gaps)}, \\
\mathcal{O}_C &: \mathcal{O}_B + \text{network, filesystem, process traces}, \\
\mathcal{O}_D &: \mathcal{O}_C + \text{hardware attestation}.
\end{aligned}

Remark 4.2 (Category error in the hierarchy). Levels \mathcal{O}_A, \mathcal{O}_B, \mathcal{O}_C are passive observation channels. Level \mathcal{O}_D is not a channel but an active attestation capability: it produces a cryptographic proof that a computation occurred in a trusted environment. We therefore split the hierarchy:

\underbrace{\mathcal{O}_A \subset \mathcal{O}_B \subset \mathcal{O}_C}_{\text{observational channels}}
\qquad
\underbrace{\mathcal{O}_D}_{\text{attestation capability}}.

4.2 The observational equivalence relation

Definition 4.3 (Observational equivalence). Two generative processes G_0, G_1 are observationally equivalent under \mathcal{O}, written G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}} G_1, if

P^{\mathcal{O}}_{G_0}(Y^n) = P^{\mathcal{O}}_{G_1}(Y^n) \quad \text{for all } n \ge 1.

4.3 Adversary class

Definition 4.4 (Adversary class). An adversary class \mathcal{A} is a set of generative processes G producing pairs (M, M') that are consistent with either

M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \quad \text{or} \quad M' \notin \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M).

We distinguish three subclasses:

· \mathcal{A}_{\text{honest}}: processes consistent with the ground-truth label.
· \mathcal{A}_{\text{conf}}: confounding processes, in which a common cause C explains the dependence.
· \mathcal{A}_{\text{sub}}: substitution processes, in which M'' \neq M' is served in place of M'.

Remark 4.5. The adversary class is not a nuisance parameter. It is a first-class argument of the identifiability question. Two problems with the same (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}) but different \mathcal{A} are genuinely different problems.

