Chapter 5. The Opacity Theorem

5.1 The theorem, conditional form

Theorem 5.1 (Opacity, conditional form). Let \mathcal{O} be an observation channel and \mathcal{A} an adversary class. Suppose there exist G_0, G_1 \in \mathcal{A} such that

G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}} G_1

but

\mathbf{1}\{ M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \}_{G_0} \neq \mathbf{1}\{ M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \}_{G_1}.

Then no certification procedure C : \mathcal{Y}^n \to \{\text{valid}, \text{invalid}\} achieves

\sup_{G \in \mathcal{A}} P_G(C(Y^n) \neq \mathbf{1}\{M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)\}) < \frac{1}{2}.

Proof. By observational equivalence, C induces the same distribution under G_0 and G_1. Since the ground-truth labels differ, the sum of error probabilities across the two processes is at least 1. Hence the worst-case error is at least 1/2. \square

5.2 Realizing the equivalence: confounding

Example 5.2 (Confounding construction). Let C be a shared pre-training corpus. Consider:

· G_0: M \to M' via T \in \mathcal{T}, with C a parent of both.
· G_1: M \leftarrow C \to M', no direct edge.

Choose parameters so that the marginal of Y = \mathcal{O}(M') is identical. Then G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}_A} G_1 but the descent labels differ.

5.3 Realizing the equivalence: substitution

Example 5.3 (Substitution construction). Let M'' be a model chosen so that P^{\mathcal{O}_A}_{M''} = P^{\mathcal{O}_A}_{M'}. Define G_0 to produce Y^n from M' and G_1 to produce Y^n from M''. Then G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}_A} G_1 but the descent labels differ.

Remark 5.4 (Detectability vs certifiability). The IRIS auditor shows that substitutions can be detected from output text in some regimes. This does not contradict the theorem. The theorem asserts that substitutions are not universally certifiable. Detectability is a property of a specific detector against a specific adversary; certifiability is a property of the channel against the class.

5.4 Scope and limits

Remark 5.5. The Opacity Theorem is almost tautological in its conditional form. Its value lies in the following three observations.

First, it localizes the failure of certification to the existence of observationally equivalent opposite-label pairs. This is a structural characterization, not a numerical one.

Second, it separates the confounding mechanism from the substitution mechanism. The empirical literature has treated them as one; they are different, and the difference matters for the design of adversaries.

Third, it makes precise what is not required for certification: universal identifiability of the model from observations. Certification can succeed even when identification fails, provided that a suitable witness exists.

