Chapter 6. The Le Cam Hierarchy

6.1 Two-point bounds

Let P_0 = P_A^{(n)} and P_1 = P_B^{(n)} be the laws of Y^n under two hypotheses. Let D : \mathcal{Y}^n \to \{0, 1\} be a test with

\alpha(D) := P_0(D = 1), \quad \beta(D) := P_1(D = 0).

Definition 6.1 (Sum risk). R_{\mathrm{sum}}(D) := \alpha(D) + \beta(D).

Proposition 6.2 (Le Cam sum bound). For any D,

R_{\mathrm{sum}}(D) \ge 1 - \Delta_n, \quad \text{where } \Delta_n = \|P_0 - P_1\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Equality is attained by the likelihood-ratio test.

Proof. Let A = \{D = 1\}. Then

\alpha(D) + \beta(D) = P_0(A) + 1 - P_1(A) = 1 + (P_0(A) - P_1(A)) \ge 1 - \Delta_n.

Equality is achieved when A is chosen to maximize P_0(A) - P_1(A), which is the likelihood-ratio event. \square

Definition 6.3 (Bayes risk). With prior (1/2, 1/2),

R_{\mathrm{Bayes}}^* := \inf_D \frac{1}{2} R_{\mathrm{sum}}(D) = \frac{1}{2} (1 - \Delta_n).

Definition 6.4 (Minimax risk). For a hypothesis family \{P_\theta\}_{\theta \in \Theta},

R_{\mathrm{minimax}}^* := \inf_D \sup_{\theta \in \Theta} P_\theta(D \neq \theta).

Proposition 6.5 (Minimax two-point bound).

R_{\mathrm{minimax}}^* \ge \frac{1}{2} \left( 1 - \inf_{\theta \neq \theta'} \|P_\theta - P_{\theta'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \right).

Proof. Fix a pair (\theta, \theta'). For any D,

\sup_\theta P_\theta(D \neq \theta) \ge \frac{1}{2} \left[ P_\theta(D \neq \theta) + P_{\theta'}(D \neq \theta') \right] = \frac{1}{2} R_{\mathrm{sum}}(D).

Apply Proposition 6.2 and take the supremum over pairs. \square

6.2 Composite hypotheses

Definition 6.6. The lineage problem has composite hypotheses:

A = \{ M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \}, \quad B = \{ M' \notin \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) \}.

Proposition 6.7 (Composite minimax bound).

R_{\mathrm{minimax}}^* \ge \frac{1}{2} \left( 1 - \inf_{P_A \in \mathcal{P}_A, P_B \in \mathcal{P}_B} \|P_A - P_B\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \right),

where \mathcal{P}_A, \mathcal{P}_B are the families of laws consistent with each composite hypothesis.

Proof. The infimum over pairs is dominated by the supremum over mixtures, and Proposition 6.5 applies to each pair. \square

6.3 The Bretagnolle–Huber refinement

Proposition 6.8. \|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{1 - \exp(-D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q))}.

This yields the refined bound

R_{\mathrm{minimax}}^* \ge \frac{1}{2} \left( 1 - \sqrt{1 - \exp\left( - \inf_{P_A, P_B} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_A \| P_B) \right)} \right).

