Chapter 8. Monotonicity of Distinguishability

8.1 Nested \sigma-algebras

Proposition 8.1. Let \mathcal{F}_n = \sigma(Y_1, \dots, Y_n) with \mathcal{F}_n \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{n+1}. Then

\Delta_n \le \Delta_{n+1}.

Proof. \Delta_n = \sup_{E \in \mathcal{F}_n} |P_A^{(n)}(E) - P_B^{(n)}(E)|. Since \mathcal{F}_n \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{n+1}, the supremum over the larger algebra dominates. \square

Corollary 8.2. \Delta_1 \le \Delta_2 \le \cdots \le 1.

8.2 Failure under non-nesting summaries

Caveat 8.3. If the verifier observes summaries S_n = \phi_n(Y_1, \dots, Y_n) with \phi_n non-injective and non-refining, monotonicity can fail. Learned digests, quantized features, and low-dimensional projections each destroy nesting.

8.3 Convergence

Theorem 8.4 (Convergence). Let \mathcal{F}_\infty = \sigma(\bigcup_n \mathcal{F}_n). Then

\Delta_n \uparrow \|P_A^{(\infty)} - P_B^{(\infty)}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Consequently:

· If P_A^{(\infty)} \perp P_B^{(\infty)}, then \Delta_\infty = 1.
· If P_A^{(\infty)} \sim P_B^{(\infty)}, then 0 \le \Delta_\infty < 1.
· If neither, then \Delta_\infty \in (0, 1) is possible.

Proof. Monotone convergence of the TV functional follows from Proposition 8.1. The limit is the TV distance on the limit algebra by definition of \mathcal{F}_\infty. The cases follow by classification of measures. \square

Remark 8.5. For product measures, Kakutani's theorem gives an explicit criterion: if P_A \neq P_B, the infinite products are singular and \Delta_\infty = 1; if P_A = P_B, they are equivalent and \Delta_\infty = 0. LLM output sequences are not product measures; the criterion must be treated case by case.

