Chapter 9. Certification and Authenticated Provenance

9.1 Relational provenance

Definition 9.1 (Provenance predicate). A provenance predicate for a transformation class \mathcal{T} is a relation

\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}} \subseteq \left( \bigsqcup_{k \ge 1} \mathcal{M}^k \times \mathcal{M} \right) \times \Pi

such that \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_1, \dots, M_k, M'; \pi) holds only if there exists T \in \mathcal{T} with

M' = T(M_1, \dots, M_k; R)

for some admissible R \in \mathcal{R}.

Remark 9.2. For k = 1, this reduces to a monogamous descent relation. For k \ge 2, it captures merging and ensembling. For chains, it composes:

\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_0, M_1; \pi_1) \wedge \cdots \wedge \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_{k-1}, M_k; \pi_k) \Rightarrow \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_0, M_k; \pi_1 \circ \cdots \circ \pi_k).

9.2 The five-property core

Definition 9.3 (Authenticated Provenance Invariant, core). An API core for \mathcal{T} is a tuple (\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}, \Pi, \mathcal{E}) where \Pi is a proof space and \mathcal{E} a family of extractors, satisfying:

(C) Conservation. For every admissible T \in \mathcal{T}_1,

\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M, T(M); \pi) = 1

for some honestly generated \pi.

(D) Discrimination. There exists a distribution \nu over non-descendants such that

\Pr_{N \sim \nu} \left[ \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M, N; \pi) = 1 \right] \le \epsilon.

Alternatively, in worst-case form:

\sup_{N \notin \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)} \Pr \left[ \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M, N; \pi) = 1 \right] \le \epsilon.

(A) Authenticity. For any probabilistic polynomial-time adversary \mathcal{A} without the witness,

\Pr \left[ \mathcal{A}(\sigma) = \pi : \mathsf{Verify}(\pi, \sigma) = 1 \right] \le \mathsf{negl}(\lambda).

(V) Verifiability. There exists a polynomial-time procedure \mathsf{Verify}(\pi, \sigma) \to \{0, 1\} with \mathsf{Verify}(\pi, \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_1, \dots, M_k, M')) = 1 for honestly generated \pi.

(E) Extractability. There exists E_{\mathcal{O}} such that for Y^n \sim \mathcal{O}(M')^{\otimes n},

\Pr \left[ E_{\mathcal{O}}(Y^n) = \mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_1, \dots, M_k, M') \right] \ge 1 - \delta.

9.3 The six-property extension

Definition 9.4 (API extended, private certification). An API extended is an API core together with:

(Z) Zero-Knowledge. There exists a simulator \mathcal{S} such that for every PPT distinguisher \mathcal{D},

\left| \Pr[\mathcal{D}(\pi) = 1] - \Pr[\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{S}(\sigma)) = 1] \right| \le \mathsf{negl}(\lambda).

Remark 9.5 (Z is a privacy refinement). The sufficiency theorem below uses only (C, D, A, V, E). Property (Z) is a privacy-preserving refinement: it ensures that verification reveals nothing beyond the validity of the provenance claim. It is not a logical prerequisite for certification. The earlier claim that zero-knowledge is "not optional" was too strong; the correct statement is that zero-knowledge is desirable for forensic viability but not required for certification in the mathematical sense.

9.4 The sufficiency theorem

Theorem 9.6 (Certifiable lineage). Suppose an API core (\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}, \Pi, \mathcal{E}) exists for (M, \mathcal{T}). Then there exists a certification procedure C : \mathcal{Y}^n \times \Pi \to \{\text{valid}, \text{invalid}\} with

\Pr[C(Y^n, \pi) = \text{valid} \mid M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)] \ge 1 - \epsilon - \delta,

\Pr[C(Y^n, \pi) = \text{valid} \mid M' \notin \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)] \le \epsilon + \delta + \mathsf{negl}(\lambda).

Proof. The verifier runs E_{\mathcal{O}}(Y^n) to obtain a candidate provenance witness, then runs \mathsf{Verify} on it. The bounds follow by union over the failure events of (E), (D), (A). \square

Remark 9.7 (Conditional). The theorem is conditional on the existence of an API. No API is constructed in this thesis for any non-trivial \mathcal{T}. Constructing one — or proving none exists — is the central open problem.

9.5 Proof infrastructure vs. certification

Remark 9.8. Recent cryptographic work — including the IETF inference chain draft and zkFinGPT — establishes proof infrastructure: the ability to prove that a model fingerprint, input, output, and execution are consistent. This is not the same as proving M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M). The former binds an execution; the latter binds a history. The distinction is the difference between attesting what is happening and certifying what has happened.

9.6 The illustrative ZKLAP

As an illustration of the kind of protocol that a future API core might take, we sketch the Zero-Knowledge Lineage Attestation Protocol (ZKLAP). The protocol uses a zk-SNARK (e.g., PLONK) with a KZG polynomial commitment to model parameters. Public inputs include the ancestor invariant hash and the transformation class identifier. Private witnesses include the model weights, the transformation delta, and the permutation equivalence map. The arithmetic circuit enforces three constraints: permutation-invariance of the invariant, membership of the delta in the transformation class, and integrity of the weight commitment.

Remark 9.9 (Two distinct problems). Even a working ZKLAP establishes only that a specific witness satisfies the circuit. The following two problems remain distinct:

\boxed{\text{Is descent mathematically defined?}}

\boxed{\text{Is the descent relation efficiently provable?}}

The first is a question about \mathcal{T}. The second is a question about \Pi. This thesis addresses only the first.

