Chapter 10. The Transformation Class Problem

10.1 Why \mathcal{T} is the crux

Until \mathcal{T} is formalized without circularity, the definitions of descent, opacity, and API are all conditional on an under-specified object. The transformation class is the primary object, not the detector.

10.2 The four requirements

R1 (Non-circular). Membership in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) must be definable without reference to "descendant."

R2 (Non-trivial). \mathcal{T} \neq \{\mathrm{id}\} and \mathcal{T} must not collapse to the full space of measurable maps \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M}.

R3 (Composition-closed). If T_1, T_2 \in \mathcal{T}, then trajectories are representable.

R4 (Realistic). \mathcal{T} contains fine-tuning, quantization, distillation, pruning, merging, recursive replication.

10.3 The unified formalism

\boxed{\mathcal{T} : \bigsqcup_{k \ge 1} \mathcal{M}^k \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{M}}

with arity k indicating the number of parents. Single-parent operations have k = 1; merging and ensembling have k \ge 2. Composition is defined on trajectories.

10.4 Candidate formalizations

Candidate 10.1 (Resource-bounded). \mathcal{T}_B consists of transformations computable in polynomial time with at most B bits of auxiliary data:

\mathcal{T}_B := \{ T : \text{poly-time}, |R| \le B \}.

This satisfies (R1)–(R3). Whether it satisfies (R4) depends on the scaling of B.

Remark 10.2 (Rejected candidate). An earlier version proposed defining \preceq by empirical fingerprinting robustness: \mathcal{T}_1 \preceq \mathcal{T}_2 iff \mathrm{AUC}(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{T}_2) \le \mathrm{AUC}(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{T}_1) for every invariant \mathcal{I}. This is circular: it defines the structure of \mathcal{T} from the performance of detectors, whereas the framework's central claim is that \mathcal{T} is prior to the detector. The candidate is rejected.

10.5 The identifiability poset

Definition 10.3. Let \mathcal{L} be a loss function on decisions and \mathcal{A} an adversary class. The optimal certification risk is

\epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) := \inf_C \sup_{G \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}_G[\mathcal{L}(C(Y^n, \pi), \mathbf{1}\{M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)\})].

Definition 10.4 (Identifiability poset). The identifiability poset is the partially ordered set

\mathfrak{I}(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) := \{ \epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}_j, \mathcal{O}_i, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) \}_{i, j}

with the order inherited from \preceq on \mathcal{T} and \subseteq on the observation hierarchy.

Remark 10.5 (Not a lattice). We deliberately avoid calling this object a lattice. A lattice requires meet and join. The domain \mathcal{T} is only partially ordered, and we have not shown that arbitrary joins and meets exist within \mathcal{T}. The correct term pending such a proof is poset or surface.

10.6 The monotonicity conjecture

Conjecture 10.6 (Structural trade-off). For \mathcal{T}_1 \preceq \mathcal{T}_2, fixed \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}:

\epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}_2, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) \ge \epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}_1, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}).

Status. Open. The conjecture is non-tautological: expanding \mathcal{T} simultaneously enlarges the descendant hypothesis (raising the difficulty of correct classification) and the negative class (raising the entropy of the decision problem). The net effect depends on the trade-off between hypothesis-volume expansion and mutual-information decay.

10.7 The observation monotonicity

Conjecture 10.8 (Observation monotonicity). For \mathcal{O}_i \subseteq \mathcal{O}_{i+1}, fixed \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}:

\epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}_{i+1}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) \le \epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}_i, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}).

This is likely provable by an argument on \sigma-algebras: a richer observation channel induces a finer \sigma-algebra, and the minimax risk is non-increasing under refinement.

10.8 Open problems

Problem 1. Construct a formalization of \mathcal{T} satisfying (R1)–(R4) with a well-characterized partial order, unifying single-parent and multi-parent operations.

Problem 2. Characterize the minimal \epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) for specified arguments, or prove no API exists.

Problem 3. Compute the asymptotic behavior of I(M; Y^n) for LLM output distributions and derive a non-trivial identification lower bound.

Problem 4. Prove or refute the structural trade-off conjecture (10.6).

Problem 5. Characterize observation channels preserving \Delta-monotonicity.

Problem 6. Compute the identifiability poset on at least a 2 \times 2 sub-block for a concrete family of models and transformations.

Problem 7. Construct an API core (\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}, \Pi, \mathcal{E}) for a specified \mathcal{T}, using state-of-the-art proof infrastructure and a formally verified extraction procedure.

Problem 8. Characterize the class of transformations for which an API core is impossible, and prove the corresponding impossibility theorem.

