Conclusion

This thesis has reformulated the problem of detecting unauthorized descendants of large language models as a problem of identifiability and certification of computational descent relative to a transformation class.

The principal findings are the following.

First, the correct notion of descent is historical: descent is what actually happened, not what could be inferred from observations, not what would change under intervention. The naive chain from historical to computational to interventional is false at its second arrow, and we have repaired the hierarchy by introducing causal-computational descent as an intermediate notion carrying an explicit dependence functional.

Second, permutation-invariance of neural network representations is captured by quotienting the parameter space by the permutation group. The quotient has a provable statistical justification via the quotient feature space.

Third, the Opacity Theorem, stated in conditional form, characterizes the failure of certification precisely: certification fails exactly when the adversary class contains observationally equivalent opposite-label pairs. The two mechanisms — confounding and substitution — are captured by the same equivalence argument and are not equivalent to each other.

Fourth, the statistical bounds for lineage certification are Le Cam bounds, not Fano bounds. Fano constrains identification, not certification. The correct hierarchy of sum, Bayes, and minimax risks, together with the composite-hypothesis extension, gives the sharp two-point characterization of the decision problem.

Fifth, monotonicity of the total-variation functional \Delta_n holds under nested \sigma-algebras and fails under non-nesting summaries. The convergence \Delta_n \uparrow \|P_A^{(\infty)} - P_B^{(\infty)}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} is the correct asymptotic statement.

Sixth, the correct object of certification is not a scalar invariant but a relational provenance predicate. A scalar invariant \mathcal{I} : \mathcal{M} \to \Sigma cannot handle multi-parent operations such as merging. The provenance predicate

\mathsf{Prov}_{\mathcal{T}}(M_1, \dots, M_k, M'; \pi)

captures single-parent, multi-parent, and chained operations under one formalism.

Seventh, the Authenticated Provenance Invariant splits into a five-property core (C, D, A, V, E) for certification and a six-property extension (C, D, A, V, E, Z) for private certification. Zero-knowledge is a privacy refinement, not a logical prerequisite.

Eighth, the primary object of study is not the detector but the quadruple (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}). The detector is downstream of the identifiability relation.

Ninth, the identifiability poset \mathfrak{I}(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) organizes the problem across observation, transformation, adversary, and loss axes. We deliberately do not call it a lattice until meet and join are shown to exist.

The central unresolved object is \mathcal{T}. Until it is formalized without circularity, under the constraint that single-parent and multi-parent operations share one formalism, neither impossibility nor sufficiency can be stated with mathematical precision.

\boxed{\text{The detector is downstream of the identifiability relation.}}

\boxed{\text{The primary object is } (\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}), \text{ not the detector.}}

The next mathematical frontier is the construction of \mathcal{T} as a well-behaved transformation class with a characterized partial order, and the resolution of the structural trade-off conjecture.

Appendices

Appendix A. Measure-Theoretic and Probabilistic Preliminaries

A.1 Total Variation Distance

Definition A.1. Let P, Q be probability measures on a measurable space (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{A}), dominated by a measure \mu with densities p = dP/d\mu, q = dQ/d\mu. The total variation distance is

\|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} := \sup_{A \in \mathcal{A}} |P(A) - Q(A)|.

Lemma A.2 (Equivalent forms). The following hold:

\|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \frac{1}{2} \|p - q\|_{L^1(\mu)} = \frac{1}{2} \int |p - q| \, d\mu.

The supremum defining \|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} is attained at A = \{x : q(x) < p(x)\}.

Proof. Let A = \{x : q(x) < p(x)\} and A^c its complement. Then

\|p - q\|_1 = \int_A (p - q) d\mu + \int_{A^c} (q - p) d\mu = P(A) - Q(A) + Q(A^c) - P(A^c)

= 2(P(A) - Q(A)) = 2 \int_A (p - q) d\mu.

For any B \in \mathcal{A},

P(B) - Q(B) = \int \mathbf{1}_B (p - q) d\mu \leq \int_{p > q} \mathbf{1}_B (p - q) d\mu = \int_A (p - q) d\mu = \frac{\|p - q\|_1}{2}.

By symmetry, Q(B) - P(B) \leq \|p - q\|_1 / 2. Combining gives the result.

A.2 Hellinger Distance and Affinity

Definition A.3. The Hellinger distance between P and Q is

h(P, Q) = \left( \int (\sqrt{p} - \sqrt{q})^2 d\mu \right)^{1/2}.

The Hellinger affinity is

\alpha_2(P, Q) = \int \sqrt{pq} \, d\mu.

Lemma A.4. \|P - Q\|_1 \leq 2 h(P, Q) and \frac{1}{2}[\alpha_2(P, Q)]^2 \leq \alpha_1(P, Q), where \alpha_1(P, Q) = \int p \wedge q \, d\mu = 1 - \|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

A.3 The Affinity Lemma

Lemma A.5. For two probability measures \mathbb{P}_0, \mathbb{P}_1 with densities p_0, p_1 with respect to \mu,

\inf_{0 \leq f \leq 1} \left[ \mathbb{P}_0 f + \mathbb{P}_1(1 - f) \right] = \alpha_1(\mathbb{P}_0, \mathbb{P}_1) = \int p_0 \wedge p_1 \, d\mu.

Proof. Let f be any test function. Then

\int (p_0 - p_1)(f - I(p_0 < p_1)) d\mu \geq 0,

since f - I(p_0 < p_1) \geq 0 when p_0 < p_1 and \leq 0 when p_0 \geq p_1. Thus

\int [p_0 f + p_1 (1 - f)] d\mu \geq \int [p_0 I(p_0 < p_1) + p_1 I(p_0 \geq p_1)] d\mu.

Equality holds for the likelihood-ratio test f = I(p_0 < p_1).

