Appendix C. Le Cam Bounds: Complete Derivations

C.1 Two-Point Bound (Sum Form)

Theorem C.1 (Le Cam). Let P_0, P_1 be two probability measures and D a test with \alpha(D) = P_0(D = 1), \beta(D) = P_1(D = 0). Then

\alpha(D) + \beta(D) \geq 1 - \|P_0 - P_1\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Proof. Let A = \{D = 1\}. Then

\alpha(D) + \beta(D) = P_0(A) + 1 - P_1(A) = 1 + (P_0(A) - P_1(A)) \geq 1 - |P_0(A) - P_1(A)| \geq 1 - \|P_0 - P_1\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Equality is attained by the likelihood-ratio test. \square

C.2 Minimax Lower Bound

Theorem C.2. For a hypothesis family \{P_\theta\}_{\theta \in \Theta},

R_{\mathrm{minimax}}^* \geq \frac{1}{2} \left( 1 - \inf_{\theta \neq \theta'} \|P_\theta - P_{\theta'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \right).

Proof. Fix a pair (\theta, \theta'). For any test D,

\sup_\theta P_\theta(D \neq \theta) \geq \frac{1}{2} [P_\theta(D \neq \theta) + P_{\theta'}(D \neq \theta')] = \frac{1}{2} [\alpha(D) + \beta(D)].

By Theorem C.1, this is at least \frac{1}{2}(1 - \|P_\theta - P_{\theta'}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}). Taking the supremum over pairs gives the claim.

C.3 Neyman-Pearson Optimality

The infimum in Theorem C.1 is attained by the likelihood-ratio test

\psi_*(z) = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } p_0(z) \geq p_1(z) \\ 1 & \text{if } p_0(z) < p_1(z) \end{cases}.

The optimal sum risk equals

P_0(\psi_* \neq 0) + P_1(\psi_* \neq 1) = \int p_0(z) \wedge p_1(z) \, dz.

This is the affinity between p_0 and p_1.

C.4 Bretagnolle–Huber Inequality

Theorem C.3. For probability measures P, Q,

\|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq \sqrt{1 - \exp(-D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q))}.

This yields the refined minimax bound

R_{\mathrm{minimax}}^* \geq \frac{1}{2} \left( 1 - \sqrt{1 - \exp\left( -\inf_{P_A, P_B} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_A \| P_B) \right)} \right).

