Appendix D. Monotonicity and Convergence of Total Variation

D.1 Monotonicity Under Nested σ-Algebras

Theorem D.1. Let \mathcal{F}_n = \sigma(Y_1, \dots, Y_n) with \mathcal{F}_n \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{n+1}. Then

\Delta_n \leq \Delta_{n+1}.

Proof. By definition,

\Delta_n = \sup_{E \in \mathcal{F}_n} |P_A^{(n)}(E) - P_B^{(n)}(E)|.

Since \mathcal{F}_n \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{n+1}, the supremum over the larger \sigma-algebra dominates the supremum over the smaller one. \square

D.2 Convergence

Theorem D.2. Let \mathcal{F}_\infty = \sigma(\bigcup_n \mathcal{F}_n). Then

\Delta_n \uparrow \|P_A^{(\infty)} - P_B^{(\infty)}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Consequently:

· If P_A^{(\infty)} \perp P_B^{(\infty)}, then \Delta_\infty = 1.
· If P_A^{(\infty)} \sim P_B^{(\infty)}, then 0 \leq \Delta_\infty < 1.
· If neither, then \Delta_\infty \in (0, 1) is possible.

Proof. Monotone convergence follows from Theorem D.1. The limit is the TV distance on \mathcal{F}_\infty by definition. The cases follow by classification of measures. \square

