Appendix E. Causal Identifiability and the Confounding Obstruction

E.1 Observational Equivalence

Definition E.1. Two structural causal models \mathcal{M}_1, \mathcal{M}_2 are observationally equivalent if they induce the same joint distribution over the observed variables.

Theorem E.2. For any structural causal model with latent confounding, there exists an observationally equivalent model without latent confounding that yields the identical joint distribution over the manifest variables.

Proof. Let P_1(A, Y, X) be the distribution under a model with latent confounding U. Define the fake conditional distribution

P_{\text{fake}}(Y(1) = y \mid X = x) = \frac{\int P(U = u \mid X = x) P(A = 1 \mid X = x, U = u) P(Y(1) = y \mid X = x, U = u) \, du}{P(A = 1 \mid X = x)}.

Substituting this into the equations governing the no-confounding model yields P_1(A, Y, X) \equiv P_2(A, Y, X).

E.2 Pearl's Backdoor Criterion

Definition E.3. A set Z satisfies the backdoor criterion relative to (X, Y) if:

1. No element of Z is a descendant of X.
2. Z blocks every path between X and Y that contains an arrow into X.

Theorem E.4 (Backdoor Adjustment). If Z satisfies the backdoor criterion, then

P(Y \mid do(X = x)) = \sum_z P(Y \mid X = x, Z = z) P(Z = z).

This establishes identifiability of the causal effect under the stated graphical conditions.

