Appendix F. Permutation-Invariance and Quotient Feature Spaces

F.1 Group Action and Quotient Construction

Definition F.1. Let V be a parameter space and G a group acting on V. Define the equivalence relation

v_1 \sim_G v_2 \iff \exists g \in G : g \cdot v_1 = v_2.

The quotient space is V/G.

Lemma F.2. \sim_G is an equivalence relation.

Proof. Reflexivity: 1 \cdot v = v. Symmetry: g \cdot v_1 = v_2 \Rightarrow g^{-1} \cdot v_2 = v_1. Transitivity: (g_2 g_1) \cdot v_1 = v_3. \square

F.2 Factorization Theorem

Theorem F.3. Let \mathcal{I} : V \to \Sigma be G-invariant. Then there exists a unique \bar{\mathcal{I}} : V/G \to \Sigma with \bar{\mathcal{I}} \circ q = \mathcal{I}, where q : V \to V/G is the quotient map.

Proof. This is the universal property of the quotient. \square

F.3 Generalization Bound via Quotient Feature Spaces

Theorem F.4 (Sannai–Imaizumi–Kawano). For invariant and equivariant deep networks, the generalization error is proportional to the square root of the volume of the quotient feature space. For DeepSets, the bound is improved by a factor of \sqrt{n!}, where n! is the number of permutations.

Proof sketch. The proof constructs a quotient feature space that measures the effect of group actions. The volume of this space controls the covering number, which in turn bounds the generalization error. The \sqrt{n!} improvement follows from the reduction in effective dimension when the group acts on n elements.

---

Appendix G. Model Merging and Fréchet Averages on Quotient Spaces

G.1 The Merging Problem

Proposition G.1. Let M' = \alpha M_1 + (1-\alpha)M_2. For a general invariant \mathcal{I},

\mathcal{I}(M') \neq \alpha \mathcal{I}(M_1) + (1-\alpha) \mathcal{I}(M_2).

Proof. Choose \mathcal{I}(M) = \|X_M\|_{\ell^2}. Then \mathcal{I}(M') = \|\alpha X_{M_1} + (1-\alpha) X_{M_2}\|_{\ell^2}, which equals the right-hand side only if X_{M_1} and X_{M_2} are collinear. \square

G.2 Quotient GeoMerge

Theorem G.2. In the presence of symmetries, the appropriate merging object is a Fréchet mean on the quotient space of parameterizations modulo symmetry. Quotient GeoMerge reduces to averaging the invariant predictors.

Proof sketch. The Fréchet mean on the quotient \mathcal{M}/G is defined as

\bar{\theta} = \arg\min_{\theta \in \mathcal{M}/G} \sum_i \alpha_i d^2(\theta, \theta_i).

Under the gauge alignment step, each input is first mapped to its orbit representative, and the Fréchet mean is computed on the quotient. The resulting predictor is the average of the invariant predictors.

G.3 Non-Linearity of Scalar Invariants

Corollary G.3. A scalar invariant \mathcal{I} : \mathcal{M} \to \Sigma satisfying \mathcal{I}(T(M)) = \mathcal{I}(M) for all T \in \mathcal{T} cannot consistently handle multi-parent operations.

Proof. This follows immediately from Proposition G.1. The invariant of a merge is not the merge of the invariants in general. \square

