Appendix H. Cryptographic Commitments: KZG Scheme

H.1 Definition

Definition H.1 (Polynomial Commitment Scheme). A polynomial commitment scheme is a tuple (\mathsf{Setup}, \mathsf{Commit}, \mathsf{Open}, \mathsf{Verify}) where:

· \mathsf{Setup}(1^\lambda) \to \mathsf{pp}: generates public parameters.
· \mathsf{Commit}(\mathsf{pp}, \phi) \to (C, d): commits to a polynomial \phi of degree \leq d.
· \mathsf{Open}(\mathsf{pp}, \phi, z) \to \pi: opens the commitment at point z.
· \mathsf{Verify}(\mathsf{pp}, C, z, y, \pi) \to \{0, 1\}: verifies that \phi(z) = y.

H.2 Security Properties

Theorem H.2 (Formally Verified Security). The KZG polynomial commitment scheme satisfies:

1. Correctness: Honest commitments verify with probability 1.
2. Polynomial binding: An adversary cannot produce two different polynomials that open to the same commitment.
3. Evaluation binding: An adversary cannot produce two different evaluations at the same point for the same commitment.
4. Hiding: The commitment reveals no information about the polynomial.

These properties have been formally verified in EasyCrypt, with proofs covering all required cases and reducing the risk of overlooked vulnerabilities.

H.3 Knowledge-Soundness

Theorem H.3. The KZG scheme satisfies knowledge-soundness under the q-Strong Diffie-Hellman assumption. For any PPT adversary that produces a valid opening, there exists an extractor that recovers the committed polynomial except with negligible probability.

This framework has been extended to a unifying framework for proving knowledge-soundness of KZG-like polynomial commitment schemes, encompassing both univariate and multivariate variants.

