Appendix I. Zero-Knowledge Proof Systems

I.1 Definitions

Definition I.1 (Zero-Knowledge). A proof system (P, V) for a language L is zero-knowledge if there exists a probabilistic polynomial-time simulator S such that for every PPT distinguisher D,

| \Pr[D(\pi) = 1] - \Pr[D(S(x)) = 1] | \leq \mathsf{negl}(\lambda),

where \pi is an honestly generated proof of x \in L.

I.2 zk-SNARK Constructions

Definition I.2 (zk-SNARK). A zero-knowledge succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge is a proof system satisfying:

· Completeness: Honest provers convince honest verifiers.
· Soundness: No PPT prover can convince the verifier of a false statement.
· Zero-Knowledge: Proofs reveal nothing beyond validity.
· Succinctness: Proof size and verification time are polylogarithmic in the witness size.
· Non-Interactivity: The proof is a single message.

I.3 Application to Model Lineage

The zk-SNARK circuit enforces three constraints:

1. Permutation-invariance: \mathcal{I}(M) = \mathcal{I}(g \cdot M) for g \in G.
2. Authorized transformation membership: \exists T \in \mathcal{T} such that M = T(M_0, \Delta W).
3. Weight integrity: The weight commitment root matches the registered provenance ledger.

The proof \pi leaks no information about the weights or the transformation delta beyond the single bit indicating that the lineage relation holds.

