Appendix J. Proofs of the Opacity Theorem

J.1 Confounding Construction

Theorem J.1. Let \mathcal{O} \in \{\mathcal{O}_A, \mathcal{O}_B, \mathcal{O}_C\}. There exist generative processes G_0, G_1 such that G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}} G_1 but the descent labels differ.

Proof. Let C be a shared pre-training corpus. Define:

· G_0: M \to M' via T \in \mathcal{T}, with C a parent of both.
· G_1: M \leftarrow C \to M', no direct edge.

Choose parameters so that the marginal of Y = \mathcal{O}(M') is identical. Then G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}} G_1, but M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) in G_0 and M' \notin \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) in G_1.

J.2 Substitution Construction

Theorem J.2. Let M'' be a model chosen so that P^{\mathcal{O}_A}_{M''} = P^{\mathcal{O}_A}_{M'}. Define G_0 to produce Y^n from M' and G_1 to produce Y^n from M''. Then G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}_A} G_1 but the descent labels differ.

Proof. By construction, the observation laws are identical. The descent status differs because M'' \neq M' and M'' is not a descendant of M. \square

J.3 Impossibility of Uniform Certification

Corollary J.3. For \mathcal{O} \in \{\mathcal{O}_A, \mathcal{O}_B, \mathcal{O}_C\}, no certification procedure achieves worst-case error less than 1/2.

Proof. By Theorems J.1 and J.2, G_0 \equiv_{\mathcal{O}} G_1. Any decision rule C induces the same distribution under both. Since the ground-truth labels differ, the sum of error probabilities is at least 1. Hence the worst-case error is at least 1/2. \square

