Appendix K. Proof Infrastructure Versus Certification

K.1 The IETF Inference Chain

The IETF draft draft-mw-spice-inference-chain defines the inference_root claim for computational provenance. It leverages ZKML proofs and TEE attestation quotes to bind a model fingerprint, input, output, and execution. This establishes proof infrastructure: the ability to prove that a specific model executed a specific inference.

This is distinct from proving M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M). The former binds an execution; the latter binds a history. The distinction is the difference between attesting what is happening and certifying what has happened.

K.2 zkFinGPT

zkFinGPT implements KZG polynomial commitments for neural network weights, binding private weights to elliptic-curve points with O(1) proof size. The work demonstrates the feasibility of ZK applied to model verification, with experimental costs including 620 seconds for proof generation on Llama-3 8B and 2.36 seconds for verification. These are proof infrastructure results, not lineage certification results.

