Appendix L. The Identifiability Poset: Formal Definition

Definition L.1. Let \mathcal{L} be a loss function on decisions and \mathcal{A} an adversary class. The optimal certification risk is

\epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) := \inf_C \sup_{G \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}_G[\mathcal{L}(C(Y^n, \pi), \mathbf{1}\{M' \in \operatorname{Desc}_{\mathcal{T}}(M)\})].

Definition L.2. The identifiability poset is the partially ordered set

\mathfrak{I}(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) := \{ \epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}_j, \mathcal{O}_i, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) \}_{i, j}

with the order inherited from \preceq on \mathcal{T} and \subseteq on the observation hierarchy.

Remark L.3. This is deliberately not called a lattice. A lattice requires meet and join operations. The domain \mathcal{T} is only partially ordered, and we have not shown that arbitrary joins and meets exist within \mathcal{T}. The correct term pending such a proof is poset or surface.

Conjecture L.4 (Structural Trade-off). For \mathcal{T}_1 \preceq \mathcal{T}_2, fixed \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}:

\epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}_2, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}) \geq \epsilon^*(\mathcal{T}_1, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{L}).

Status. Open. The conjecture is non-tautological: expanding \mathcal{T} simultaneously enlarges the descendant hypothesis and the negative class. The net effect depends on the trade-off between hypothesis-volume expansion and mutual-information decay.

