Appendix M. Counterexamples

M.1 Computational Descent Does Not Imply Interventional Descent

Counterexample M.1. Let T(M; R) = N, where N \in \mathcal{M} is fixed and independent of M. Then M' = N \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) for every M, but the law of M' is constant in M, so the interventional effect of M on M' is null.

M.2 Interventional Descent Does Not Imply Computational Descent

Counterexample M.2. Let C be a common cause of M and M'. Then M' \in \operatorname{Desc}^{\mathrm{int}}_{\mathcal{T}}(M) if interventions on M propagate through C, but M is not an argument of any transformation producing M'.

M.3 Non-Linearity of Scalar Invariants Under Merging

Counterexample M.3. Let \mathcal{I}(M) = \|X_M\|_{\ell^2}. For M' = \alpha M_1 + (1-\alpha)M_2,

\mathcal{I}(M') = \|\alpha X_{M_1} + (1-\alpha) X_{M_2}\|_{\ell^2} \neq \alpha \|X_{M_1}\|_{\ell^2} + (1-\alpha) \|X_{M_2}\|_{\ell^2}

unless X_{M_1} and X_{M_2} are collinear.

M.4 Failure of Monotonicity Under Non-Nesting Summaries

Counterexample M.4. Let S_n = \phi_n(Y_1, \dots, Y_n) with \phi_n non-injective and non-refining. Then \mathcal{F}_n^S \not\subseteq \mathcal{F}_{n+1}^S in general, and \Delta_n^S need not be monotone. Learned digests, quantized features, and low-dimensional projections each destroy nesting.

