Appendix N. Auxiliary Results

N.1 Data-Processing Inequality for f-Divergences

Theorem N.1. For any f-divergence D_f and any Markov kernel K,

D_f(PK \| QK) \leq D_f(P \| Q).

This is used in the proof of Fano's inequality and in the derivation of the data-processing floor.

N.2 Kakutani's Theorem for Product Measures

Theorem N.2 (Kakutani). Let \{P_i\} and \{Q_i\} be sequences of probability measures. The infinite product measures \prod_i P_i and \prod_i Q_i are either mutually singular or equivalent. They are equivalent if and only if

\sum_i \left( 1 - \int \sqrt{p_i q_i} \, d\mu_i \right) < \infty,

where p_i = dP_i/d\mu_i, q_i = dQ_i/d\mu_i.

This applies to the convergence analysis of \Delta_n for product measures.

N.3 Hellinger Affinity Bound

Lemma N.3. For any P, Q,

1 - \|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq \sqrt{1 - h^2(P, Q)}.

This relates the total variation distance to the Hellinger distance and is used in the derivation of the Bretagnolle–Huber inequality.

